Q: Get the playPause function to switch icons in the browser A: <p>Font Awesome kits replace your <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> tags with <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> elements by default.</p>
<p>Because of this SVG replacement, <code>ctrlIcon.classList.contains(&quot;fa-pause&quot;)</code> fails after the first click because the <code>ctrlIcon</code> reference to the original <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> tag is lost or modified in the DOM.</p>
<hr />
<p>Here is your fixed javascript:</p>
<pre><code>let progress = document.getElementById(&quot;progress&quot;);
let song = document.getElementById(&quot;song&quot;);
let ctrlIcon = document.getElementById(&quot;ctrlIcon&quot;);

song.onloadedmetadata = function(){
    progress.max = song.duration;
    progress.value = song.currentTime;
};

function playPause(){
    // Check the song's actual state instead of the icon's class
    if(!song.paused){
        song.pause();
        // Target the element again dynamically to support SVG replacement
        let icon = document.getElementById(&quot;ctrlIcon&quot;);
        icon.classList.remove(&quot;fa-pause&quot;);
        icon.classList.add(&quot;fa-play&quot;);
    } else {
        song.play();
        let icon = document.getElementById(&quot;ctrlIcon&quot;);
        icon.classList.add(&quot;fa-pause&quot;);
        icon.classList.remove(&quot;fa-play&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or, you can just log into your Font Awesome account and switch the technology from Webfont to SVG. I but honestly think this script is a better solution.</p>

Q: What technical limitations prevent using just the address-of operator (&amp;) without pointer variables in C? A: <ol>
<li><p>Dynamic memory allocation.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>char *c = malloc( n );
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Passing arrays to functions, and returning arrays from functions.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f( Type *p ) { ... }

Type a[4];
f( a );
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Operating on different objects without making copies.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f( Type *p ) { ... }
f( g() );
f( h() );
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>Type *p = cond() ? g() : h();
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Returning values through arguments.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f( Type *out ) { *out = ...; }
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>

Q: How to bind dynamic DataTable to DataList? A: <p>Since you created the data columns dynamically at runtime, you have to add them to the gridview at runtime as well, ex:</p>
<pre><code>DataTable dt = new DataTable();
dt.Columns.Add(new DataColumn(&quot;Rego No&quot;, typeof(string)));
foreach (var item in list)
{ 
    dt.Columns.Add(new DataColumn(string.Format(&quot;{0:dd/MM}&quot;,item),typeof(string)));
    //enter code here 

    dlcalender.Columns.Add(string.Format(&quot;{0:dd/MM}&quot;,item), string.Format(&quot;{0:dd/MM}&quot;,item));
}
dlcalender.DataSource = dt;
dlcalender.DataBind();
</code></pre>
<p>In the above code, I have added the column to the datalist in teh same time it is added to the data table.</p>
<p>You can filter them if you don't want to add all of it.</p>

